    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\n A positional class pattern crashes when an attribute lookup specified by `__match_args__` causes an exception other than `AttributeError`. The problem lies in Cython's `__Pyx__MatchCase_ClassPositional` implementation, since it handles a failed call to `PyObject_GetAttr()` only when the exception is `AttributeError`; in all other cases, the returned object remains `NULL` and execution proceeds into the normal success pathway.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n```cython\nclass R:\n    __match_args__ = ('a',)\n\n    @property\n    def a(self):\n        raise ValueError('boom')\n\n\ndef probe():\n    match R():\n        case R(x):\n            return 'matched'\n\n\nprobe()\n```\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\nWhen a ValueError occurs during a lookup of a positional attribute, it should be propagated in a manner consistent with CPython; the code should then raise the error `ValueError: boom` rather than terminating with a segmentation fault.\n\n### OS\n\nUbuntu-24.04 (WSL)\n\n### Python version\n\n3.12.3\n\n### Cython version\n\n3.3.0\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_",
